Here is an example of generating an OpenAPI spec for Form Request objects in a Laravel application without using macros or external packages:

```php
// The name of the command
protected $signature = 'openapi:generate';

// The description of the command
protected $description = 'Generate an OpenAPI specification for all routes in the application';

public function handle()
{
    // Initialize the OpenAPI specification
    $openApiSpec = [
        'info' => [
            'title' => 'My Laravel API',
            'version' => '1.0.0',
        ],
    ];

    // Get all of the defined routes in the application
    $routes = Route::getRoutes();

    // Iterate over the routes and generate their request and response definitions
    foreach ($routes as $route) {
        // Use reflection to determine the Form Request class for the route
        $controllerMethod = $route->action['controller'];
        $reflectionMethod = new ReflectionMethod($controllerMethod);
        $requestClass = $reflectionMethod->getParameters()[0]->getType()->getName();

        // Generate the request definition
        $requestProperties = (new ReflectionClass($requestClass))->getProperties();

        // Add the request definition to the OpenAPI specification
        $openApiSpec['paths'][$route->uri] = [
            strtolower($route->methods[0]) => [
                'parameters' => $requestProperties,
            ],
        ];
    }

    // Output the generated OpenAPI specification
    $this->line(json_encode($openApiSpec, JSON_PRETTY_PRINT));
}
```

This example uses PHP reflection to determine the Form Request class for each route and generates the request definition by reflecting on the properties of the Form Request class. The request definition is then added to the OpenAPI specification for the route. Finally, the generated OpenAPI specification is outputted to the command line.

Here is an example implementation of the `OpenApi` class:

```
class OpenApi
{
    // The info section of the OpenAPI specification
    private $info;

    // The paths section of the OpenAPI specification
    private $paths;

    public function __construct(array $info)
    {
        $this->info = $info;
        $this->paths = [];
    }

    public function addPath(string $path, array $operations)
    {
        $this->paths[$path] = $operations;
    }

    public function toArray()
    {
        return [
            'openapi' => '3.0.2',
            'info' => $this->info,
            'paths' => $this->paths,
        ];
    }
}
```

This class models an OpenAPI specification, providing methods to add paths and operations to the specification. It also includes a method to convert the specification into an array format, which can be used to output the OpenAPI specification in formats such as JSON.

