To find the length of the longest substring without repeating characters in JavaScript, you can use the following code:

```javascript
function lengthOfLongestSubstring(s) {
  let charMap = {};
  let start = 0;
  let maxLength = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
    let char = s[i];
    if (charMap[char] >= start) {
      start = charMap[char] + 1;
    }
    charMap[char] = i;
    maxLength = Math.max(maxLength, i - start + 1);
  }

  return maxLength;
}
```

This algorithm employs a sliding window technique to track the current substring and its unique characters. A hash map (`charMap`) is used to store the last index of each character in the substring. The `start` variable indicates the starting index of the current substring, while `maxLength` records the length of the longest substring found. For each character in the input string, the algorithm checks if it has already appeared in the current substring. If so, the starting index is updated to the index after the last occurrence of the duplicate character. The character's index is updated in the hash map, and the current substring's length is calculated and compared to the maximum length found. The result is the length of the longest substring without repeating characters.

