Here is an example of a C++ function that performs a similar operation to the `numpy.partition()` function in Python:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> partition(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {
    std::nth_element(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + k, arr.end());
    return std::vector<int>(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + k);
}
```

This function takes a reference to a `std::vector` of integers and an integer `k`, and returns a new `std::vector` containing the first `k` elements of the input vector, in the order they appear in the input vector. The `std::nth_element()` function is used to rearrange the input vector so that the element at the kth position is in its final sorted position, with all elements before it being less than or equal to it, and all elements after it being greater than or equal to it.

It is important to note that while `numpy.partition()` returns an array with elements partitioned around the kth element, this C++ function returns only the first k elements of the input vector.

Here's an updated version of the C++ function that uses the introsort algorithm to sort the input vector before partitioning it:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> partition(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {
    // Sort the input vector using introsort
    std::sort(arr.begin(), arr.end(), [](int a, int b) {
        return a < b;
    });

    // Get the first k elements from the sorted vector
    return std::vector<int>(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + k);
}
```

In this version, the `std::sort()` function is used to sort the input vector, which typically employs the introsort algorithm. Introsort is a hybrid sorting algorithm that primarily uses quicksort and switches to heapsort when the recursion depth exceeds a certain limit, thereby avoiding quicksort's worst-case time complexity of O(n^2).

Additionally, `std::nth_element` and `std::partial_sort` can also be used for similar purposes, as they efficiently sort only the first `k` elements of the array, leaving the rest in an unspecified order.

Here's an updated version of the C++ function that uses the `std::nth_element()` and `std::partial_sort()` algorithms to sort the input vector before partitioning it:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> partition(std::vector<int>& arr, int k) {
    // Use nth_element to ensure the kth element is in its final position
    std::nth_element(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + k, arr.end(), [](int a, int b) {
        return a < b;
    });
    // Use partial_sort to sort the first k elements
    std::partial_sort(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + k, arr.end(), [](int a, int b) {
        return a < b;
    });

    // Return the first k elements from the sorted vector
    return std::vector<int>(arr.begin(), arr.begin() + k);
