```java
import com.google.firebase.auth.FirebaseAuth;
import com.google.firebase.auth.FirebaseToken;
import org.springframework.security.authentication.UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken;
import org.springframework.security.core.Authentication;

public class FirebaseAuthenticationProvider {
    private final FirebaseAuth firebaseAuth;

    public FirebaseAuthenticationProvider(FirebaseAuth firebaseAuth) {
        this.firebaseAuth = firebaseAuth;
    }

    public Authentication getAuthentication(String idToken) {
        FirebaseToken decodedToken = firebaseAuth.verifyIdToken(idToken);
        String uid = decodedToken.getUid();
        return new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(uid, null, null);
    }
}
```

In this code, necessary libraries from Firebase and Spring Security are imported. The `FirebaseAuthenticationProvider` class is defined to take a `FirebaseAuth` instance as a parameter in its constructor. This instance is used to verify the ID token provided by the client.

The `getAuthentication` method accepts the ID token as a parameter and uses the `verifyIdToken` method on the `FirebaseAuth` instance to decode and verify the token. If the token is valid, the user's UID is extracted from the decoded token and returned in a new `UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken` object. The other parameters of the `UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken` constructor are set to `null` in this example.

This code serves as a basic example of generating an authentication object using Firebase ID tokens. It is important to implement additional checks and error handling to ensure the security and reliability of the authentication process.

