In this example, two buttons allow switching between the tabs. Each tab is represented by a separate component, and the `{#if}` block is used to conditionally render the components based on the `activeTab` variable. When `activeTab` matches a specific tab, the corresponding component is displayed.

To create a test using Playwright, you need to install Playwright and import it into your test file. You can then utilize the `playwright` object to initiate a new browser session and navigate to a specified URL. Once the page is loaded, Playwright's methods can be used to interact with the page and perform assertions to ensure the page operates as intended.

Here is an example of a test using Playwright:
```
// Import Playwright
const { playwright } = require("playwright");

// Define the URL to test
const url = "https://example.com";

// Define the test
describe("Example page", () => {
  let browser, page;

  // Launch a new browser and navigate to the URL
  beforeAll(async () => {
    browser = await playwright.chromium.launch();
    const context = await browser.newContext();
    page = await context.newPage();
    await page.goto(url);
  });

  // Close the browser when the test is finished
  afterAll(async () => {
    await browser.close();
  });

  // Define the test case
  test("Page title is correct", async () => {
    // Get the page title
    const title = await page.title();

    // Verify that the page title is correct
    expect(title).toBe("Example page");
  });
});
```
In this code, Playwright is used to open a new browser, navigate to the specified URL, and execute a test case to check if the page title is correct. Additional test cases can be added, and Playwright's methods can be employed to interact with the page and perform assertions to verify its functionality.

