3. Assign a name to your function and select a programming language. Netlify supports languages such as JavaScript, TypeScript, Go, and Rust.
4. Develop your function code in the editor. Netlify Edge functions are similar to regular serverless functions but are executed at the edge of Netlify's network, closer to the user.

Here is an example of an Edge function in JavaScript that adds a custom header to the response:
```javascript
// Example Netlify Edge function in JavaScript

exports.handler = function(event, context, callback) {
  const response = event.response;
  response.headers['X-Custom-Header'] = 'Custom Value';
  return callback(null, response);
}
```
This function adds a custom header to the response object before returning it.

5. After writing your function code, click on the "Save and Deploy" button to deploy it.
6. Once deployed, you can test the function by sending a request to its URL, which will be in the format `https://your-site-name.netlify.app/.netlify/functions/your-function-name`.

Netlify Edge functions have certain constraints, such as a maximum execution time of 10 seconds and a memory limit of 128 MB. They are suitable for lightweight, fast, and globally distributed logic that needs to run closer to the user.

