In simpler terms, an interface in programming is like a blueprint that outlines certain methods and properties a class must have. It sets a standard for how different objects can interact, even if they work differently under the hood.

For example, in C#, `IList<T>` is an interface that outlines what a list should be able to do. However, you can't create an object directly from an interface because it's just a set of rules, not a complete object.

To use an interface, you need a class that follows its rules. The `List<T>` class is one such class that implements the `IList<T>` interface, meaning it provides the actual code for the methods and properties defined by the interface.

Here's an example to clarify:
```csharp
// Define a class that implements the IList<T> interface
class MyList<T> : IList<T>
{
    // Implement the required methods and properties here
}

// This will cause a compiler error because you can't create an instance of an interface directly
IList<string> myList = new IList<string>();

// This creates an instance of the MyList<T> class, which implements the IList<T> interface
MyList<string> myList = new MyList<string>();

// This creates an instance of the List<T> class, which also implements the IList<T> interface
List<string> myList = new List<string>();
```
In your code, you should replace `new IList<FailureMessage>()` with `new List<FailureMessage>()` to create a new list that follows the `IList<FailureMessage>` interface rules.

