To set the `href` and `download` attributes of an anchor (a) element using jQuery, you can use the `attr` method. Here's an example:

```php
<a id="downloadCSV" href="#">Download CSV</a>

<script>
$(document).ready(function() {
  $('#downloadCSV').click(function() {
    // Create a CSV data string
    var csvData = "Name,Email\nJohn Doe,john.doe@example.com\nJane Smith,jane.smith@example.com";

    // Encode the CSV data as a data URI
    var csvUrl = "data:text/csv;charset=utf-8," + encodeURIComponent(csvData);

    // Set the href and download attributes of the anchor element
    $(this).attr({
      'href': csvUrl,
      'download': 'data.csv'
    });
  });
});
</script>
```

In this example, clicking on the anchor element with ID `downloadCSV` will create a CSV data string, encode it as a data URI, and set the `href` and `download` attributes of the anchor element to the data URI and file name respectively.

The `attr` method takes an object with the attribute names and values as key-value pairs. In this case, the `href` and `download` attributes of the anchor element are set using the `attr` method.

The `download` attribute specifies the name of the file to be downloaded. In this example, it is set to "data.csv". You can update the file name as desired.

It is important to note that not all browsers support the `download` attribute, and some may not support the `data` URI scheme. Providing a fallback option for users whose browsers do not support these features is advisable.

To set the `href` and `download` attributes of an anchor (a) element individually using jQuery chaining, you can utilize the `attr` method. Here's an example:

```php
<a id="downloadCSV" href="#">Download CSV</a>

<script>
$(document).ready(function() {
  $('#downloadCSV').click(function() {
    // Create a CSV data string
    var csvData = "Name,Email\nJohn Doe,john.doe@example.com\nJane Smith,jane.smith@example.com";

    // Encode the CSV data as a data URI
    var csvUrl = "data:text/csv;charset=utf-8," + encodeURIComponent(csvData);

    // Set the href and download attributes of the anchor element using chaining
    $(this)
      .attr('href', csvUrl)
      .attr('download', 'data.csv');
  });
});
</script>
```

In this example, chaining is used to set the `href` and `download` attributes of the anchor element sequentially. The `attr` method can be invoked multiple times on the same jQuery object to achieve this chaining effect.

The sequence of the `attr` method calls is not crucial as long as they are applied to the same jQuery object. However, it is often considered good practice to set the `href` attribute first, followed by the `download` attribute.

It is important to note that chaining in jQuery is primarily a syntactical convenience and does not inherently improve performance compared to setting the attributes separately.

