In this example, a button with the ID "openBtn" is used to trigger the modal window. When the button is clicked, the jQuery code opens a modal window with the ID "iframeModal" and displays the iframe inside it.

You can use CSS to style the modal window and iframe to meet your design requirements and ensure it appears as a popup.

There are several reasons why a modal window might display scrollbars when it is not intended to. One possibility is that the modal or the iframe has fixed dimensions, and the content within the iframe exceeds these dimensions. To address this, ensure that both the modal and the iframe are designed to be responsive, allowing them to adjust according to the content size.

Another reason could be incorrect CSS configuration for the modal or iframe, which might not be set to hide scrollbars. You can try adding the following CSS to hide the scrollbars:

```css
#iframeModal {
  overflow: hidden;
}

#iframeModal iframe {
  overflow: hidden;
}
```

This code will prevent scrollbars from appearing, even if the content inside the iframe is larger than the modal's dimensions.

Additionally, consider setting the iframe's height and width to 100% to ensure it fills the modal window:

```html
<iframe src="http://example.com" style="height:100%; width:100%;"></iframe>
```

It is also important to check if the iframe's content is responsive. If it is not, you might need to use CSS media queries to make it responsive.

