`htons` is a function found in the `<arpa/inet.h>` header file in C that converts a 16-bit value from host byte order to network byte order. This function is commonly used in the context of Internet socket addresses, which require network byte order.

Byte order refers to the sequence in which bytes are arranged within a value. Most modern computers use little-endian byte order, where the least significant byte (LSB) is stored at the lowest memory address. Conversely, network byte order is big-endian, meaning the most significant byte (MSB) is stored at the lowest memory address.

For example:
```c
#include <arpa/inet.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    unsigned short host_port = 0x1234;  /* host byte order */
    unsigned short net_port;            /* network byte order */

    /* convert host byte order to network byte order */
    net_port = htons(host_port);

    printf("Host byte order: 0x%x\n", host_port);
    printf("Network byte order: 0x%x\n", net_port);

    return 0;
}
```
The output of the program would be:
```vbnet
Host byte order: 0x1234
Network byte order: 0x3412
```
