 skb\_pull(skb, (64 - dword\_align\_bytes));
 if (rsi\_prepare\_beacon(common, skb)) {
 rsi\_dbg(ERR\_ZONE, "Failed to prepare beacon\n");
 return -EINVAL;
 }
 skb\_queue\_tail(&common->tx\_queue[MGMT\_BEACON\_Q], skb);
 rsi\_set\_event(&common->tx\_thread.event);
 rsi\_dbg(DATA\_TX\_ZONE, "%s: Added to beacon queue\n", \_\_func\_\_);

 return 0;
}

The resource leak bug in the code is that the `skb` variable is not being freed in the case where `rsi_prepare_beacon()` returns an error. This can be fixed by adding a call to `kfree_skb()` to free the `skb` in this case, like so:

```c
static int rsi_send_beacon(struct rsi_common *common)
{
    struct sk_buff *skb = NULL;
    u8 dword_align_bytes = 0;

    skb = dev_alloc_skb(MAX_MGMT_PKT_SIZE);
    if (!skb)
        return -ENOMEM;

    memset(skb->data, 0, MAX_MGMT_PKT_SIZE);

    dword_align_bytes = ((unsigned long)skb->data & 0x3f);
    if (dword_align_bytes)
        skb_pull(skb, (64 - dword_align_bytes));
    if (rsi_prepare_beacon(common, skb)) {
        rsi_dbg(ERR_ZONE, "Failed to prepare beacon\n");
        kfree_skb(skb);
        return -EINVAL;
    }
    skb_queue_tail(&common->tx_queue[MGMT_BEACON_Q], skb);
    rsi_set_event(&common->tx_thread.event);
    rsi_dbg(DATA_TX_ZONE, "%s: Added to beacon queue\n", __func__);

    return 0;
}
```

This will ensure that the `skb` is properly freed when `rsi_prepare_beacon()` returns an error, avoiding the resource leak.

In Angular, there are several methods to store a JWT token, each with its own considerations:

1. Local Storage: You can use the `localStorage` API to store the JWT token in the browser's local storage.
```javascript
// To set the token in local storage
localStorage.setItem('jwtToken', token);

// To get the token from local storage
const token = localStorage.getItem('jwtToken');
```
Note: Storing JWT tokens in local storage can have security implications. It is important to use HTTPS and implement additional security measures.

2. Cookies: JWT tokens can also be stored in cookies.
```kotlin
// To set the token in a cookie
import { CookieService } from 'ngx-cookie-service';

constructor(private cookieService: CookieService) {}

this.cookieService.set('jwtToken', token);

// To get the token from the cookie
const token = this.cookieService.get('jwtToken');
```
Note: Use secure and httpOnly flags when setting cookies to enhance security.

3. Session Storage: The `sessionStorage` API allows storing the JWT token in the browser's session storage.
```vbnet
// To set the token in session storage
sessionStorage.setItem('jwtToken', token);

// To get the token from session storage
const token = sessionStorage.getItem('jwtToken');
```
Note: Session storage is cleared when the browser window is closed, which may not be suitable for persisting tokens across sessions.

4. State Management Libraries: Libraries like NgRx or Redux can store the JWT token in the application's state.
```typescript
// To set the token in the NgRx store
import { Store } from '@ngrx/store';
import { setJwtToken } from './auth.actions';

constructor(private store: Store) {}

this.store.dispatch(setJwtToken({ jwtToken: token }));

// To get the token from the NgRx store
import { selectJwtToken } from './auth.selectors';

constructor(private store: Store) {}

this.store.select(selectJwtToken).subscribe(token => {
  // Do something with the token
});
```
Note: State management libraries can be useful for managing complex application states but may add complexity to the codebase. Consider using them if your application state is complex.

