To transition from using the `@StreamListener` annotation to the functional model in Spring Cloud Stream, you can utilize the `MessageHandler` functional interface. This interface includes a single method, `handleMessage(Message<?> message)`, which you can implement to specify the logic for processing incoming messages.

Here is an example of converting a `@StreamListener` method to the functional model:
```
@Bean
public MessageHandler messageHandler() {
    return message -> {
        // handle message logic here
    };
}
```
You can also use a lambda expression for a more concise implementation:
```
@Bean
public MessageHandler messageHandler() {
    return message -> System.out.println(message.getPayload());
}
```
After defining the `MessageHandler`, use the `@Bean` annotation to register it in your Spring application. You can then bind it to a specific input channel using the `bindTo()` method.
```
@EnableBinding(Sink.class)
public class MyStreamProcessor {

    @Bean
    public MessageHandler messageHandler() {
        return message -> {
            // handle message logic here
        };
    }

    @StreamEmitter
    public void emit() {
        // emit message logic here
    }

    @StreamListener(Sink.INPUT)
    public void handle(String payload) {
        // handle message logic here
    }
}
```
In this setup, the `messageHandler()` method is registered as a bean and can be bound to the input channel specified by `Sink.INPUT`. The `handle` method is no longer necessary, as the message handling logic is now contained within the `messageHandler()` method.

In Spring Cloud Stream, the `@StreamListener` annotation is used to handle incoming messages on a specific input channel, with the option to filter messages using a `condition` attribute based on a SpEL expression.

To achieve similar functionality using the functional model, you can define a `MessageHandler` bean that encapsulates the logic of the `@StreamListener` method. A separate `MessageFilter` bean can be created to implement the filtering logic, using the `MessageSelector` functional interface. This interface requires implementing the `accept(Message<?> message)` method to determine if a message should be processed.

Here is an example of replacing a `@StreamListener` method with a condition attribute using the functional model:
```java
@Bean
public MessageFilter filter() {
    return message -> {
        // filter logic here
        return true; // or false
    };
}

@Bean
public MessageHandler messageHandler() {
    return message -> {
        // handle message logic here
    };
}
```
You can then connect the filter and handler:
```java
@Bean
public IntegrationFlow flow() {
    return IntegrationFlows.from("input")
            .filter(filter())
            .handle(messageHandler())
            .get();
}
```
In this setup, the `filter()` method is a `MessageFilter` bean implementing the `MessageSelector` interface, and the `messageHandler()` method is a `MessageHandler` bean for processing messages that pass the filter.

For a more concise approach, lambda functions can be used:
```java
@Bean
public MessageFilter filter() {
    return message -> message.getHeaders().containsKey("type");
}
```
In this example, the `filter()` method checks if the message headers contain the key "type." If present, the message proceeds to the `messageHandler()` for processing; otherwise, it is discarded.

